  <section aria-labelledby="notes-h">
    <h3 id="notes-h">Good to know</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Emergency rooms cannot turn you away.</strong> EMTALA requires a medical screening exam and stabilizing treatment regardless of ability to pay &mdash; but it does not cover ongoing care, and bills can still follow you (see charity care below).</li>
        <li><strong>Hospital charity care is a right at qualifying incomes.</strong> California law makes uninsured and high-medical-cost patients at or below 400% FPL eligible for a hospital's charity care or discount payment policy, and hospitals cannot consider your monetary assets when determining that eligibility.</li>
        <li><strong>Short-term plans are not an option in California.</strong> State law has prohibited them since January 1, 2019, and federal rollbacks of short-term rules do not revive them here.</li>
        <li><strong>Health care sharing ministries are not insurance.</strong> They can exempt you from California's penalty, but they do not guarantee payment and are not regulated as coverage.</li>
        <li><strong>Medicare removes the work question entirely</strong> &mdash; people entitled to or enrolled in Medicare are not subject to the Medicaid community engagement requirement.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
